        "spearman_rho": 0.015582407471138756,
        "caveat": "AMBULATORY != INPATIENT. The published rise in HbA1c testing over the 2000s is an AMBULATORY quality-measure phenomenon (Medicare beneficiaries with diabetes: ~74% -> 84% over 2003-2012; A1c testing in hospitalised patients WITHOUT diabetes: 5.2% -> 8.8% over 2007-2009). Neither window matches 1999-2008 and neither population matches this variable, which measures inpatient ordering behaviour during an admission and is driven by hospital protocol and endocrinology staffing. Strack et al. (2014), the canonical paper on this dataset, report HbA1c measured in 18.4% of encounters and perform NO temporal-trend analysis, so there is no within-dataset published trend to anchor against. This anchor is therefore confirmatory only and cannot by itself change the verdict.",
        "role_note": "DEMOTED to supporting: the published trend is ambulatory, this variable is inpatient ordering behaviour; a flat result here is uninformative, a decline would be evidence against H1"
